 pretty  interesting so the way you configure  envoy at runtime is through something  called discovery services and together  they're called aggregated discovery  services or 80s so listen our discovery  service what ports should I be listening  to and how should I be listening to them  discovery services how should i route  traffic as it comes in and goes cluster  and endpoints you can almost think of  them as services and pods in the  kubernetes lingo so a cluster is a  service in kubernetes and endpoints or  pods or endpoints with in kubernetes and  they can discover that at runtime live  they don't have to be restarted the  proxy doesn't have to be restarted which  now brings us to sto sto is just an open  source implementation of a service mesh  you don't need sto as long as you have  something like that it still gives you  uses those envoy proxies and gives you  all that kind of network functionality  and from an architectural perspective  what it looks like is something like  this and for the past few minutes we've  been talking about the data plane how  the actual data flows back and forth so  in this particular case every service  even if it's outside of kubernetes will  run with a out of process proxy so in  this case this is the Envoy proxy that's  running right below each service and the  service will never talk directly to  another service it will go through its  sidecar proxy through the other sidecar  proxy and then back up and then it's  between the proxies is where all the  networking magic and all those advanced  feature functionality in telemetry all  of that is happening and services have  no clue of the networking topology much  like everything else we need a data  plane which we talked about in a control  plane the control plane consists of  three components the pilot the mixer and  the Citadel and let's talk about each of  these at very high level the pilot is  the deployment or the service that  actually configures those Envoy proxies  so it talks to every single Envoy in  your service mesh and says this is how  you're constructed this is how you need  to talk to each other it has kind of a  north sight on the south side and we  kind of talked about the south side  which talks to the to the envoy API so  it uses the those aggregated discovery  services to configure the envoys itself  and then on the north side it uses you  know other service discovery services  like it uses the kubernetes api to  discover all the services running it  also uses sto configs which are custom  resource definitions to actually where  you can configure yourself the route  rules where you can say I want you know  50% of the traffic going to version one  and 50% to version two it discovers all  of that and then pushes that down to to  the envoys themselves so it's  responsible for just configuring the  Envoy proxies mixer is kind of the  pluggable  it does two things it does policy and  quota enforcement and it does telemetry  collection itself it's just a collector  it's not a log logging solution or a  metric solution it's an API that exposes  these metrics out to something right so  from a log from an observability  perspective those proxies by the way are  sitting at a very fortunate position  because they get to see every single  request to go through them and they can  asynchronously send all these fancy  metrics to something in this case that  something is the mixer and then you can  plug in your own solutions and your own  tooling to app to abstract I'll leave  all of these you know metrics and then  display them however you want so for  example Prometheus has a plugin which  comes as a part of an sto implementation  prometheus is a metrics collector that  connects through mixer collects all the  metrics and then you can put some  dashboarding solutions like de fauna or  something else stackdriver has a plugin  where you can do all of those things in  in a single place it also does policy  and quota enforcement so you can have  you know you can have rate limiting and  and so on and so forth and then Citadel  is the last piece and that does all the  security stuff right so it provides  verifiable identity in the form of  spiffy IDs and then it also provides a  mutual encryption so the the Citadel  list sort sort of like a CA or a  certificate authority where it's  responsible for distributing  certificates revocation and you know  collecting that so this is where I just  want to show you kind of an example of  how this kind of all works and let's  pray to the demo gods that everything  everything is kind of working on that  can we please switch to demo I was told  not to have screen savers okay so just  to kind of set the stage on where we are  we have three clusters three kubernetes  clusters they're all running in GCP we  have two clusters one in central one in  east that's going to kind of simulate  our multi cluster multi cloud deployment  and then we have a third cluster that's  just running our spinnaker so it's just  responsible for just sending  applications down there I'm just going  to show you just an example of a web app  it's a very simple web app that takes  the version tag from the application  name and puts it back into the  index.html file so we can just always  see what's running and then we  can just curl that application so if we  just do and I'm just gonna pick one of  those two clusters but it's essentially  the exact same thing so if we look at  the deployments on the central cluster I  have two deployments running so I always  have a canary running and then I have my  prod running and the version changes  depending upon your the version next is  always canary in the version now is prod  and I always have four pods of of prod  and one pod of canary running so what  I'm gonna do right now is just start a  kind of a curl loop so you can just kind  of see what's what's going on so  currently in my GK central cluster I  have in my app which is just a web  front-e